      "writtenOn": "2017-06-01T11:43:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is Marius design and code but I will take a stab at it.\n\n\u003e why 4 bits are used instead of 1 for each increment?\n\nI don\u0027t have the definitive answer, 2 reasons I know at least:\n- it\u0027s more robust against single/double bit flips which is the usual flash flakiness (particularly if a write is interrupted)\n- at least the flash technology usually has physical limitations, we can only write a cell so many times between erases.\nHere I\u0027m reading \"A given word can only be programmed twice between erase cycles, irrespective of whether the programmed value is 0 or 1.\", which might or might not match (the word here is 32 bits not a byte I imagine).\n\n\u003e what happens when the counter overflows (or is this not going to happen - what\u0027s the max value?)\n\nwe return 0xffffffff and stays there\nsee:\n \tif (hi \u003d\u003d PAGE_WORDS \u0026\u0026 lo \u003d\u003d PAGE_WORDS) {\n\t\t/* We are exhausted, can count no more */\n\t\treturn -1;\n\t}\n\nif I\u0027m counting properly, the max value with 2kB pages should be:\n- per LO page: 2048*8/4 increments \u003d\u003d 4096 values\n- per HI page: 2048*8/(2*4) LO pages \u003d\u003d 2048 LO pages\nso a max value of 4096 * 2048 \u003d 8388608\n\nFor the U2F application, as far as I can tell, it\u0027s a 4-byte counter and the rollover behavior is not defined (but 8M authentications is a large number, ie it\u0027s 2 auth/min during the entire 6-year lifespan of the chromebook)",
      "parentUuid": "cbc65e1c_6a8c5847",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 11,
        "endChar": 46
